og roll is a type of soft bun (bread roll) shaped specifically to contain a hot dog or another type of sausage."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-hot-dog-bun"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_dog_bun"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Hot dog","dish/hot-dog"]]],["Pacific Northwest cuisine","cuisine/pacific-northwest-cuisine",[["Seattle-style hot dog","dish/seattle-style-hot-dog"]]],["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states",[["Seattle-style hot dog","dish/seattle-style-hot-dog"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Vienna sausage","ingredient/vienna-sausage"],["Bialy","ingredient/bialy"],["Bialy (bread)","ingredient/bialy-bread"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"],["Hot dog","ingredient/hot-dog"],["Sautéed onions","ingredient/sauteed-onions"]]}]},"ingredient/sago":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sago","n":"Sago","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Sago is a starch extracted from the soft, spongy core tissue of several tropical palm species, especially Metroxylon sagu. For centuries, it has been an important traditional staple food among many indigenous communities across the islands of Southeast Asia and the Melanesian region, including Malaysia, Indonesia, and Papua New Guinea. In different regions, sago is also known by local names such as saksak (Papua New Guinea), rabia (Maluku Islands), and sagu (Indonesia and Malaysia)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sago"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sago"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Kapurung","dish/kapurung"],["Papeda (food)","dish/papeda-food"],["Tekwan","dish/tekwan"]]],["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine",[["Lekor",